# Environment for the hostpinger service, read by systemd before it starts. Changes take effect
# on: systemctl restart hostpinger
#
# Settings that belong to the application itself — ping interval, timeout, database size limit —
# are edited on the Configuration page and stored under /var/lib/hostpinger, not here.

# Address and port the web UI listens on. The default accepts connections from any interface;
# use http://127.0.0.1:8080 to restrict it to the machine itself. Note that opening the port in
# firewalld is a separate step the package deliberately does not take:
#   firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080/tcp && firewall-cmd --reload
ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080

# The .NET runtime opens a debugger transport and a diagnostics socket as it starts, neither of
# which an installed service has any use for: PrivateTmp= in the unit already puts both somewhere
# dotnet-counters and dotnet-dump cannot reach without entering the service's namespace, and on a
# confined system the runtime being refused its own debug pipe is logged as an SELinux denial.
# This stops all three — transport, socket and profiler — being created at all.
#
# Comment it out on a machine where you intend to attach something, or set the narrower
# DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics_Debugger=0, which drops the debugger but keeps the socket.
DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=0

# The Hosts page and the graph tooltips convert timestamps to local time on the server, so this
# decides what users see. Unset means the system time zone.
#TZ=Europe/Berlin

# Overrides for where state is kept. Both default to /var/lib/hostpinger, which is what
# StateDirectory= in the unit provisions; changing them means granting the service write access
# to the new location yourself.
#Pinger__DatabasePath=/var/lib/hostpinger/hostpinger.db
#Pinger__UserSettingsPath=/var/lib/hostpinger/usersettings.json
